            <ArticleTitle>The lumbosacral electromyographic screen: revisiting a classic paper.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2219-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Knutsson (Acta Orthopaed Scand (1961) 1) published a classic thesis comparing lower limb electromyography (EMG) results with intra-operative findings for 206 patients with sciatica. This unique and detailed work presented a rare opportunity to again use this data and determine the optimal number of muscles in a screening examination; a different study aim to that of the original investigator.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data from Knutsson (1961) were used to determine the identification rates and sensitivities with respect to clinical and intra-operative diagnostic standards, for various muscle screens.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Selected 4 muscle EMG screens identified over 97% of EMG confirmed radiculopathies and over 89% of surgically confirmed radiculopathies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These findings underscore the utility of 4 muscle EMG screens in the evaluation of patients with suspected lumbosacral radiculopathy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
